#![TODO]
//! TODO (wado-compiler bug): linking two modules that each define a recursive
//! type used behind a closure makes the WIR pipeline emit an invalid core Wasm
//! module — `panic!("Internal compiler error: WIR pipeline generated invalid
//! core Wasm module ... type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found (ref
//! null $type)")` at `codegen.rs`'s `validate_core_module`. The two modules'
//! same-named-but-distinct recursive `Node` types are conflated when the
//! closure-taking `run_entry` is monomorphized/linked across them.
//!
//! Minimal, Kiln-independent reduction of the ICE first seen compiling two
//! Gale-generated parsers into one module (each parser carries a recursive
//! `CstNode` and a `_run_parse_entry(entry: fn(&mut Parser))`). Remove the
//! `#![TODO]` once the WIR pipeline keeps the two recursive types distinct.
//!
//! Pinned to `-O0`: this minimal reduction ICEs at `-O0`, where the WIR bug is
//! direct. At `-O2` the optimizer elides the trivial closure / empty `Node`, so
//! the reduction compiles clean there — but the full generated parsers (which
//! the optimizer can't elide) still ICE at `-O2`.

use a from "./sub/xmod_recursive_closure_ice_a.wado";
use b from "./sub/xmod_recursive_closure_ice_b.wado";

test "two modules with a recursive type behind a closure compile to invalid wasm" {
    assert a::parse().kids.len() == 0;
    assert b::parse().kids.len() == 0;
}

__DATA__
{
    "test": {},
    "only_opt": ["O0"]
}
